  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/185393f07b920bf9b72799f1c17c4245017b500ff1ca92a0104fe95ce621bde4.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=9,$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 4+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =9-4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5}).$",
